    "markdown": "---\ntitle: scatter\n---\n\n\n\n```python\nscatter(\n    data,\n    x,\n    y,\n    year=None,\n    *,\n    color=None,\n    size=None,\n    fit='ols',\n    log_x=False,\n    log_y=False,\n    title=None,\n    ref=None,\n)\n```\n\nTwo variables against each other in one year, one point per unit.\n\n## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}\n\n| Name   | Type        | Description                                                                                                                                        | Default    |\n|--------|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|\n| data   |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` (and ``year``), e.g. from ``load``                                                                        | _required_ |\n| x      | str         | The numeric columns on the horizontal and the vertical axis                                                                                        | _required_ |\n| y      | str         | The numeric columns on the horizontal and the vertical axis                                                                                        | _required_ |\n| year   |             | The year to plot (default: the last with data for ``x``), or ``\"all\"`` to pool every year (one point per unit and year)                            | `None`     |\n| color  | str \\| None | Colour the points by a column (categories, or a numeric variable on a continuous scale) or by a coarser scale of the units, whose names are added  | `None`     |\n| size   | str \\| None | A non-negative numeric column that sets the area of the points                                                                                     | `None`     |\n| fit    | str \\| None | The line drawn: ``\"ols\"`` (least squares, with its slope, R² and the correlation written on the figure), ``\"lowess\"`` (a local smooth) or ``None`` | `'ols'`    |\n| log_x  | bool        | Logarithmic axes; the line and the numbers then use natural logarithms, and units with a value of zero or less are left out                        | `False`    |\n| log_y  | bool        | Logarithmic axes; the line and the numbers then use natural logarithms, and units with a value of zero or less are left out                        | `False`    |\n| title  | str \\| None | The figure title                                                                                                                                   | `None`     |\n\n## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}\n\n| Name   | Type          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n|--------|---------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n|        | ScatterResult | A result with ``.df`` (the units plotted: ``geo_id``, ``name`` and the columns used), ``.fig`` (the unit names show on hover), ``.slope``, ``.intercept``, ``.r2`` and ``.p_value`` (of the least-squares line of ``y`` on ``x``, whichever ``fit`` is drawn), ``.corr`` (Pearson), ``.n`` (units), ``.year``, ``.notes`` and ``.interpret()``. |\n\n## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}\n\n```python\n!pip install -q geo4bolivia\nimport geo4bolivia as geo\n\nres = geo.scatter(\"sdg\", \"urbanization\", \"index_sdg\",\n                  color=\"department\", size=\"population\")\nprint(res.interpret())\nres.fig\n```\n\n::: {.g4-example-links}\n[Open a blank Colab notebook to run it](https://colab.research.google.com/notebooks/empty.ipynb)\n:::\n\n\n::: {#5a0b59a5 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}\nAcross 343 units in 2024, **Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100)** and **Urbanization rate (% of population)** are positively correlated (r = 0.56, a moderate association): units with higher Urbanization rate (% of population) tend to have higher Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100).\n\nThe least-squares line has a slope of 0.094: one more unit of Urbanization rate (% of population) goes with 0.094 higher Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100), on average. The line accounts for 31% of the variation in Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100) across units (R² = 0.31); the slope is statistically significant at the 1% level, by a test that treats the units as independent of each other.\n\n_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._\n:::\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n```{=html}\n<div style=\"height:525px; width:100%;\">            <script src=\"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mathjax/3.2.2/es5/tex-svg.min.js\"></script><script>if (window.MathJax && window.MathJax.Hub && window.MathJax.Hub.Config) {window.MathJax.Hub.Config({SVG: {font: \"STIX-Web\"}});}</script>                <script>window.PlotlyConfig = {MathJaxConfig: 'local'};</script>\n        <script charset=\"utf-8\" src=\"https://cdn.plot.ly/plotly-4.1.1.min.js\" integrity=\"sha256-O24V1F27f8pb0glCkelh3cVHLNiHAJ5gCaVtq2aNch8=\" crossorigin=\"anonymous\"></script>                <div id=\"fc43e83c-09ac-4dd7-b859-389b1eba9493\" class=\"plotly-graph-div\" style=\"height:100%; width:100%;\"></div>            <script>                window.PLOTLYENV=window.PLOTLYENV || {};                                if (document.getElementByI